Annotation Event Handler
--------------------------

Views can use annotations on `Node` fields to automatically wire JavaFX event handlers to the Controller.
This removes the need for boilerplate `addEventHandler` calls.

The available annotations include `@OnMouse`, `@OnKey`, `@OnAction`, `@OnSwipe`, `@OnDrag`, `@OnRotate`, and `@OnTouch`.
Each annotation specifies the event sub-type to listen for.

For example, annotating a `Button` field with `@OnMouse(Mouse.Clicked)`:

```java
@OnMouse(Mouse.Clicked)
public Button defaultCommand;
```

- Field annotation — framework wires the node to a Controller handler at View init.
- `@OnMouse(Mouse.Clicked)` — fires on single click; see `@OnKey`, `@OnAction`, etc.


When the View is initialized, the framework scans all annotated fields, resolves the matching Controller handler method, and attaches the JavaFX event handler automatically.

The Controller method name is derived by convention: `on` + *fieldName* + *EventType*.
For instance, a field named `myButton` with `@OnMouse(Mouse.Clicked)` will call `controller().onMyButtonMouseClicked(MouseEvent)`.

If no field-specific name is needed, the annotation uses a default handler. In the example above with `defaultCommand`, the handler is `onMouseClicked(MouseEvent)` in the Controller.

Here is the annotation used in the sample View:


