### linkUi

`linkUi` wires a JavaFX event to attach another Model's node into a placeholder in the current View:

```java
linkUi(view().getContentPane(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, DetailModel.class,
    WBuilder.waveData(JRebirthWaves.ATTACH_UI_NODE_PLACEHOLDER, view().getDetailHolder()));
```

- `linkUi(node, event, ModelClass, waveData...)` — on event, attaches target Model's node into the placeholder.
- Uses built-in **Attach Ui** wave group; always processed on JAT.


### linkService

`linkService` wires a JavaFX event to call a Service method:

```java
linkService(view().getRefreshButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED,
    DataService.class, DataService.DO_LOAD_DATA);
```

- `linkService(node, event, ServiceClass, WaveType)` — fires `returnData` when the event occurs.
- Equivalent to calling the service from the Model, but wired declaratively from the Controller.


All `link*` methods accept an optional `Callback<E, Boolean>` parameter that acts as a gate — the action is performed only when the callback returns `true`.


Adapters & Handlers
--------------------------

Controllers can implement **adapter interfaces** that provide a full set of event-handling methods for a given event family. Each adapter interface has a corresponding **handler** class that delegates events to the adapter methods.

Available adapter / handler pairs:

| Adapter Interface | Handler Class | Event Family |
|-------------------|---------------|--------------|
| `MouseAdapter`    | `MouseHandler`    | Mouse events (clicked, pressed, released, moved, dragged…) |
| `KeyAdapter`      | `KeyHandler`      | Key events (pressed, released, typed) |
| `ActionAdapter`   | `ActionHandler`   | ActionEvent |
| `DragAdapter`     | `DragHandler`     | Drag events (detected, over, dropped, entered, exited…) |
| `RotateAdapter`   | `RotateHandler`   | Rotate gesture events |
| `SwipeAdapter`    | `SwipeHandler`    | Swipe gesture events |
| `TouchAdapter`    | `TouchHandler`    | Touch events |

To use an adapter, have the Controller implement the interface (or extend a `Default*Adapter`) and override only the methods you need. Then register the adapter with `addAdapter()`:


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.sample.ui;

import javafx.scene.input.MouseEvent;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.exception.CoreException;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.AbstractController;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.adapter.DefaultMouseAdapter;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.wave.WBuilder;
import org.jrebirth.af.sample.command.SampleCommand;
import org.jrebirth.af.sample.command.SamplePoolCommand;
import org.jrebirth.af.sample.command.SampleUICommand;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

/**
 * The class <strong>SampleController</strong>.
 *
 * @author
 */
public final class SampleController extends AbstractController<SampleModel, SampleView> {

    /** The class logger. */
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SampleController.class);

    /**
     * Default Constructor.
     *
     * @param view the view to control
     *
     * @throws CoreException if an error occurred while creating event handlers
     */
    public SampleController(final SampleView view) throws CoreException {
        super(view);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initEventAdapters() throws CoreException {

        // Manage Ui Command Button
        linkCommand(view().getUiCommand(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, SampleUICommand.class);

        // Use the inner class
        addAdapter(new SampleMouseAdapter());

    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initEventHandlers() throws CoreException {
        // Listen events

        // Manage Pooled Command Button
        view().getPooledCommand().setOnMouseClicked(getHandler(MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED));
    }

    /**
     * Manage Mouse click of widget that have annotation.
     *
     * @param event the mouse event
     */
    void onMouseClicked(final MouseEvent event) {

        LOGGER.debug("MouseClicked => Call Sample Command");

        // Manage Default Command Button
        model().getCommand(SampleCommand.class).run();

    }

    /**
     * The class <strong>SampleMouseAdapter</strong>.
     */
    private class SampleMouseAdapter extends DefaultMouseAdapter<SampleController> {

        @Override
    // ...
```

The inner class `SampleMouseAdapter` demonstrates a custom adapter that overrides `mouseClicked`:


```java
    private class SampleMouseAdapter extends DefaultMouseAdapter<SampleController> {

        @Override
        public void mouseClicked(final MouseEvent mouseEvent) {
            super.mouseClicked(mouseEvent);

            LOGGER.debug("MouseClicked => Call Sample Pool Command");

            model().sendWave(WBuilder.callCommand(SamplePoolCommand.class));
        }

    }
```

The handler is then attached to the node. For example, to get the MouseHandler for `MOUSE_CLICKED`:

```java
view().getPooledCommand().setOnMouseClicked(getHandler(MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED));
```

- `getHandler(EventType)` — returns the shared handler that delegates to registered adapters.
- Attach after nodes exist — typically in `initEventHandlers()`.


Controller Annotations
--------------------------

As an alternative to adapters or manual wiring, Controller methods can be annotated with `@OnAction`, `@OnMouse`, `@OnKey`, etc. to receive specific events by convention.

This is the Controller-side counterpart to the View field annotations described earlier. The framework scans annotated methods and wires them to the appropriate View nodes.


Direct Event Handling
--------------------------

For complete control, you can use `addHandler(node, EventType, handler)` to attach any custom JavaFX `EventHandler` to a node. This is useful for one-off event handling that doesn't fit the adapter or annotation patterns.

Full sample Controller
--------------------------

Here is the complete sample Controller showing all three approaches — `linkCommand`, adapters, and direct handlers:


```java
