Another example from the Undo/Redo showcase, demonstrating `linkCommand` with additional `WaveData`:


```java
public final class UndoController extends DefaultController<UndoModel, UndoView> {

    /** The class logger. */
    private static final Logger LOGGER = LoggerFactory.getLogger(UndoController.class);

    /**
     * Default Constructor.
     *
     * @param view the view to control
     *
     * @throws CoreException if an error occurred while creating event handlers
     */
    public UndoController(final UndoView view) throws CoreException {
        super(view);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initEventAdapters() throws CoreException {

        // Manage Ui Command Button
        linkCommand(view().getUndoButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, UndoCommand.class, UndoModel.stackName);
        linkCommand(view().getRedoButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, RedoCommand.class, UndoModel.stackName);

        linkCommand(view().getAddCircleButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, CreateShapeCommand.class, WBuilder.waveData(UndoAppWaves.SHAPE_TYPE, ShapeType.Circle), UndoModel.stackName);
        linkCommand(view().getAddSquareButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, CreateShapeCommand.class, WBuilder.waveData(UndoAppWaves.SHAPE_TYPE, ShapeType.Square), UndoModel.stackName);
        linkCommand(view().getAddRectangleButton(), MouseEvent.MOUSE_CLICKED, CreateShapeCommand.class, WBuilder.waveData(UndoAppWaves.SHAPE_TYPE, ShapeType.Rectangle), UndoModel.stackName);

    }

}
```


SimpleModel
=============

When the UI is simple or generated entirely in code, creating separate View and Controller classes is unnecessary boilerplate.
JRebirth provides `DefaultSimpleModel` (and `DefaultSimpleObjectModel<N, O>`) for this use case.

With a SimpleModel:

* There is no separate View or Controller class.
* The root node is created directly according to the generic type.
* UI initialization happens in `initSimpleView()` instead of a View's `initView()`.
* Event handling can be done directly inside the model or with inline JavaFX handlers.

This pattern is ideal for leaf UI components, tab content, generated layouts, or quick prototypes.


```java
public class SimpleModel extends DefaultSimpleObjectModel<Text, String> {

    @Override
    protected void initSimpleView() {

        node().setText(object());

    }

}
```

- `DefaultSimpleObjectModel<Text, String>` — node type `Text`, bound object type `String`.
- `initSimpleView()` — replaces separate View class; `node()` is the auto-created root.


In this example, `DefaultSimpleObjectModel<Text, String>` creates a `Text` node automatically. The `initSimpleView()` method sets the text value from the bound object — no View or Controller needed.

To use a SimpleModel, declare it with its node type:

```java
public class MySimpleModel extends DefaultSimpleModel<VBox> {

    @Override
    protected void initSimpleView() {
        node().getChildren().addAll(
            new Label("Hello"),
            new Button("Click me")
        );
    }
}
```

- `DefaultSimpleModel<VBox>` — root node type only; no bound business object.
- Ideal for leaf panels, tabs, or prototypes without full MVC boilerplate.


Or with a bound business object:

```java
public class ItemModel extends DefaultSimpleObjectModel<Label, Item> {

    @Override
    protected void initSimpleView() {
        node().setText(object().getName());
    }
}
```

- `object()` — current bound `Item`; refresh UI when the model object changes.
- Combine with `bind()` overrides on `DefaultSimpleObjectModel` for property-driven updates.


